  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe aim to improve the current record by refining the parent solution (n=480) and testing related parent candidates at various resolutions. The core idea includes:\n1. Using a more sophisticated initialization by exploring structured templates (Fourier bases, sinc functions, and optimized pulses) beyond random/parent perturbations\n2. Implementing a multi-resolution strategy with targeted optimization steps for capturing complex behavior\n3. Enhancing constraint handling through validated, separable methods\n4. Focusing on 2-point correlation structure optimization\nThe main limitations are the smooth_max_and_grad approximation and the need for careful constraint projection. We hypothesize that optimized sinc-like pulses and more holistic search could yield better C bounds.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent h: [0,2] -> [0,1] as a sample array of size n with \u2211h[i] = n/2.\nObjective: C5 = max_{k\u2208[-2,2]} integral_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nTranslates to max of calculated correlation across lags, scaled by dx=2/n.\nConstraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i and \u2211h[i] = n/2.\n\nFor the core optimization:\nVariables: h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\nObjective: min C5 (capped if necessary)\nConstraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n/2\nWe use a customized Lagrangian framework with gradient projection.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\nStage 1: Initialization\n- Input: Seed, Budget, parent_h_values (if available)\n- Generate templates at base resolution (e.g., 360):\n  1. Parent interpolation (if available)\n  2. Multi-frequency sine mixtures (4-8 terms per candidate)\n  3. Sinc-like pulse-centered (Dirichlet, Hamming window, etc.)\n  4. Asymmetric double-hump profiles\n- For each template, perform initial descent (method=\"SLSQP\")\n\nStage 2: Template-driven refinement\n- Use projected Newton steps on the constraint functions:\n  - Compute dual variables based on maximum violation\n  - Project components in the null space of the constraint Jacobian\n  - Apply shrinkage to avoid boundary effects\n\nStage 3: Lag analysis & punctured descent\n- Identify critical lags (top 10 peaks in correlation)\n- For each lag, perform localized coordinate ascent\n- Use step condition: f(x + t\u03c3) > f(x) for 0 < t < \u03b5\n\nStage 4: Recursive budget allocation\n- Schedule resolution trials for n=360, 480, 720, 960\n- Allocate iteration counts based on resolution (higher n: more iterations)\n- Maintain parent solution across cycles\n\nSearch choices:\nInitiate with:\n- n_list: [360, 480, 720, 960] (even indices)\n- Sine components: amplitudes 0.2-0.5, frequencies 1-3\n- Window sizes: 3-15 points for sinc-like functions\nPerturbation uses gradient-based with adaptive step (0.01-0.05)\nLocal optimization: 500 steps of conjugate gradient\n\nBudget management:\n- Time budget tracking across resolutions\n- For n=360: 300s\n  n=480: 500s\n  n=720: 300s\n  n=960: 300s\n- Abort if time<30s/phases\n\nValidation:\n- Final projection to sum constraint (with Lagrangian steps)\n- Cross-check top-5 lag C5 values for completeness (store as hists)\n- Return complex wins schema: {C_bound, h.astype(bool), lcm_achieved}\n- Use direct double-check correlation computation\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Verify final C5 computation uses same dx=2/n\n- Check n/2 == sum(h) with tolerance\n- Special case: normalized size of h should be \u2248 n/2\n- Compute multiple C5 estimates (top-30 lags)\nImplementation details for projection:\nFor projection to sum= n/2:\n  residual = n/2 - np.sum(h)\n  Work vector = h (copy) (clamped to [0,1] first)\n  while abs(residual) > 1e-3 * n/2 and iterations < 500:\n      distribution = function of distance to bounds (tentative: exponential)\n      h[dir] += residual; residual -= adjustment based on h dir clamp\n      Clamp h after update\n  Global constraint is separable: optimize pairs (x,y) independently\n\nComplex win surface projection:\nCreate a loss landscape: L = C5 + \u03b1*(max(|h[i]|,|1-h[i]|))\nUse multi-stable loss: L_param = sum_h(\u03bb(h[i]-0.5)^2 + sigmoid(x) penalty)\nOptimize with analytical gradients to avoid numerical issues\n\nComplexity: O(n^2) time (gradient calculation) but O(n) work with smart indexing\nMemory: All operations are in-place with minimal extra storage",
